WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Goal!

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:06.360
The soccer invasion has spread to your living room!

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:29.360
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.060
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.160
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:39.020
Guys, we usually start by talking about like, I would do like, hey, so what do you guys think about soccer?

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.040
And we'd laugh about it and we'd make some cute jokes or whatever.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.000
But we're not going to do that this time, I promise.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:54.480
Instead, we are going to actually start with a listener question because somebody took the time to write in to us at www.nostalgiacast.com.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.120
You just hit the little contact button, fill out the question.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.860
It goes to an email that I can't give you like I don't know the domain.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.840
So just anyway, that's how we get the emails.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.980
And then this is the like, only fourth time this has ever happened.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.120
So we're going to answer this question live.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.700
I can't wait. All right.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.140
Late to Gaming writes,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:23.260
After playing through so much of the NES library, are there certain genres that have you surprised or maybe are more enjoyable than you expected?

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:29.180
What about genres you normally don't like, but have found NES games in the genre that you've really enjoyed?

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.220
Sean, you first.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.140
Wow. Okay.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:40.120
So the first question is regarding genres that I did not expect to like, correct?

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:41.780
Correct. All right.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:52.680
I'd say that I was always kind of dreading at the beginning of this podcast, just how many shmups that we'd have to play.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:02:02.920
Um, and cause like, I just never really was a fan and, uh, had a, I just thought they were overly simplistic and yada, yada.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:15.940
Uh, but, um, and I'd still say that they're not my favorite, but I would say that, uh, playing, playing through them on this podcast has shown me that, uh, that they can be done well.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:28.040
And it's like, there is a lot of creativity that goes into differentiating, um, a good shmup from the crowd, even though the crowd is, is pretty thick.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:35.720
Um, so yeah, I'd say like that, that's, that's where I would, that's where I'd place that.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.280
And then for the second question, if you have an answer for it, it's fine.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:45.280
If you don't, uh, genres you normally don't like, but have found an NES game that you do like in that genre.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:56.920
Oh man, it's, it's, it's almost the same, um, with like, you know, Gradius and, uh, the, the several other, uh, shmups that we've put onto the essential games list.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:11.640
Um, yeah, I, I, I guess also, um, there, like the, the contra types, like the, the running guns, I, I are also in the same vein cause they're kind of almost the same genre.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.540
Um, but there's been some, there's been some good stuff in there too.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:17.840
Joe, how about you?

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:33.040
Well, as far as, um, genres that I didn't think I would like, or, or, or that surprised me, I don't know if you'd even consider this a specific genre, but like going all the way back to Duck Hunt, I mean, just like Zapper games in general.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:44.740
I played Duck Hunt when I was a kid, but it's one of those things where like, I hadn't played it in so long and I just did not expect the, I did not expect the Zapper to be so accurate.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.300
So I, and I also really loved Freedom Force because of that same thing.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:04:00.380
And I, I think that that surprised me, um, because I thought I was just kind of misremembering, you know, or like my memory of when I was a kid was just like, oh, this is fun because I'm shooting at things and there's feedback.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.760
But like who knew how accurate I was really being?

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.500
Um, now I realize how, um, how cool that really is.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:22.880
And, and also like, I guess this is a genre, I'm really being a liberal with the term genre and what it means, but like, this is a type of game that I didn't really realize existed on the NES, but like was not something that would have sounded like an appeal to me on paper.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:31.180
But we have, we've had games like Golgo 13 and like Bayou Billy, both of which were not like great overall.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:40.820
Like they're not like that, like not our favorite games, but like just the, these games on the NES that we keep kind of running into that try to do everything, which is a flaw.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:48.780
But like, to me, to me, it's like, it's just really interesting how they tried to do it all and how they incorporated it, incorporated it into the gameplay.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:56.920
So like, similarly, like there's, there's not a, there's not a zapper point in Golgo 13, but there's like a first person shooting part.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.780
And like you, I would think that that sounds awful.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.920
It's actually kind of functional and it's kind of fun.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:18.760
And like, you know, maybe I'm not, uh, maybe I'm still kind of like marrying the, uh, just the interest I have in like how those games are made and how they like, how they found like workarounds to problems and stuff to like, I don't want to be confused.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:25.440
Using that to like, was, did I have a lot of fun with it, but like just something about those games, whenever I, whenever we get something like that, it always kind of fascinates me.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:40.340
And I'd say for myself, you know, I'm kind of surprised at, you know, the few sports games, uh, that we played that I've liked, you know, um, I, I don't hate sports games in general, but I wasn't expecting older sports games to be any good.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.500
You know, it's like, we have the modern day solutions to this kind of stuff.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.160
What could the NES games bring us?

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:56.340
But if we're expanding the definition of sports games, you know, you got golf, lunar pool, RBI baseball, you know, and those are just the ones that aren't even on the essential games list.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.720
Forget the three or four that we do have on the essential games list.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:08.100
I'm really surprised at how they can feel so different because of their eight bit limitations to sports games today that I don't really feel like the comparison matters.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:15.860
RBI baseball and baseball stars are just as interesting, um, in terms of, uh, you know, baseball games today.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.120
Like they're just doing something completely different.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.480
They scratch a different itch.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:31.900
So, uh, sports games in general, I think, are something that like have negative, uh, connotations in the video game world just because they become super cheap afterwards and they don't matter because they're yearly installments.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:40.100
But I, I'd say, you know, relook at some of these if you haven't, if you skipped over them, the episodes, because you thought that, uh, you played them long ago and there's nothing new to explore there.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.760
They do offer something different.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.860
And what a great segue because we have another sports game today.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:56.360
It's our first soccer game since, well, soccer by Nintendo, uh, back in the, uh, the original launch, uh, the black box games.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.080
That was the only other soccer game we've had so far.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:07.920
So rather than call it soccer as well, or something confusing like football, they decided to call it goal, uh, which I think is exciting.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.520
Keep in mind, this is a Jaleco game.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.500
It's another game in the Jaleco sports series of things.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.440
They also make, uh, bases loaded.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:23.840
Uh, they're making a few of those, but, you know, we've noticed that the one standout for Jaleco games is the presentational aspect of it.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:31.680
The way that they make the sports try to, uh, you know, have a little more realism to them or a little more, uh, simulation involved in them.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:37.640
Would you guys say that you felt that with goal as well as the other Jaleco games?

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:38.920
Definitely. Oh yeah.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:50.680
I remember, um, like when I first put it together or sorry, the first time I turned it on and just saw that they were going for this like faux isometric style.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:59.320
Um, I was like, okay, this is definitely going to be a different experience than the much more, uh, primitive soccer.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.200
Um, which definitely had a more arcadey feel.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.540
So yeah, I'd say that I could kind of tell that this was going to be a different experience.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:16.120
Yeah. I feel like so far with like most sports games, at least like, uh, something that, that would control similar, like a hockey game or a soccer game.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:28.200
Um, there's these features that kind of help to make it less of a simulation and more of a, more of a game where it's like, when you want to pass to this player, like somehow you aim at that player and you pass and then the ball will go to that player.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:34.180
Like, uh, maybe I'm even thinking of more modern games where it's like, okay, I, I, I see who I want to pass to.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:39.640
I'm going to pass and my player will pick it to, to the, to the teammate I want to pass to this.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:43.300
It's like, you have to actually aim in the direction you want to pass.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:09:04.480
You have to actually aim in like the direction you want to shoot and like, and then you, and then you have to control the receiver and actually get the ball, which like, I feel like is something about it is like looser, but more realistic because of that, which I don't know I'm getting ahead of us here, but like, that's like the first thing, at least that I noticed when I play this game is like, it feels a lot more like fluid than other soccer games.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:12.280
That's one of the, the, the main points that I wanted to bring up too, if we wanted to move into like general game feel or gameplay.

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:14.620
Yeah, I think we should talk about gameplay.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:24.100
Uh, I just wanted to mention just because I've been saying Jaleco over and over again, that it's important to note that, you know, they're the publisher and slash developer things of these games.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:37.560
But the real developer of these games is that ghost company Tose that we keep hearing about over and over again, who just like, don't put their name on anything, you know, like you won't see them on the credits, but they're making, uh, these games.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:45.060
But I would say Jaleco is more involved in these sports line series of games and, and how they all kind of take on a similar feel.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:10:00.880
Yeah. And, um, one, one thing that I, I always kind of have in the back of my head when it comes to a soccer game is, uh, like, cause FIFA is pretty like codified now.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:06.740
And I, I know that, um, it's, I think it's one of the most successful game series in the world.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:14.420
So, uh, what the, the legacy of, of older games, like, I didn't really know where that started.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:18.620
Like, was it with the first EA soccer game or where did that start?

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:31.380
And one of the things that I think is, uh, one of the defining features of a FIFA game is just the, the, the feel that the ball is a separate entity than the player.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:42.220
And the fact that, uh, your movement is going to be dependent on like, does it make sense that you kicking a soccer ball, like, can you turn on a dime with that?

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:43.220
Like, no, you can't.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:53.880
Um, and with soccer, soccer, like the original soccer game that we played on NES, it very much felt like a hockey game where it just sort of, the ball is taped to your feet.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:11:00.080
Um, whereas in this, like, it actually felt like I was trying to corral this thing.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:16.300
And especially with passing, um, that sort of aftertouch of, um, you directly changing to, um, the character that you're passing to before the ball even gets to you.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:23.660
Um, that definitely gives it like a much more simulation feel like how Joe was saying before.

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:30.400
Right. The manual describes those as hooking and slicing shots, which I'm sure are the correct terminology and everything.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:36.760
I'm just not familiar with soccer, but that is an inclusion, uh, on this kind of soccer game that I'm, I'm impressed with here.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:58.000
You know, it's something that first you just decide to whether you want to, you know, pass or shoot or whatever, but then you can change the direction of the ball doing a hook shot or a slice shot after the fact, almost an aftertouch where you hold down the B button and press the, uh, you press the up button to give the ball a forward spin, uh, the down button to give the ball back spin.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:02.100
And that extends or shortens the length, but you can also hook a little bit.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.280
It's this way it curves and has, has a nice touch to it.

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:09.420
And I think where we are with this game is Sean, you really summed it up.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:19.760
Well, with that, the ball is kind of its own character, Joe, you were talking about maybe what some people would describe as the frustrations of not being able to auto pass.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:28.840
Uh, you see other players around you and you want to pass to them and the ball just kind of has a mind of its own based on where the player is facing.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.580
But to me, that makes more sense, right?

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:35.520
Yeah. I want to say I was not considering that a negative or a frustration.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:47.280
No, no, I know. I'm saying, I think people would think that's a problem at first is that they aim for the particular character and the ball just kind of, especially with a pass one, you know, it doesn't have nearly as much power as a kick.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:52.400
And it doesn't necessarily land in front of anything that you might want it to land to.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:13:19.000
I'm of two minds on it because, uh, I, I think the, uh, the fact that it, you, your, what are your pat, what I, what I was trying to bring attention to was sort of the, what is now kind of a natural feel in a sports game is like when you pass the, the game almost intuits, uh, depending on your directional input, like where you're trying to put it.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:29.260
Not quite like exactly what your input was, but like, oh, there's, there's a, uh, I'm detecting another, uh, player in this area.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:32.320
And this, and the player has put this input in.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:36.240
So this pass is going to go kind of in that direction.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:43.240
Whereas like, no, like if you, um, it will go exactly where you point the D pad.

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:45.340
And I'm not saying that, I don't think that that's great.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:52.820
I think that there, uh, could have been some sort of pass assist on.

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:59.040
Um, but I, I also think that the fact that it gives you control of the,

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:04.660
player you're passing to immediately, like that was, that was what I was praising.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:12.520
Whereas the kind of, uh, slapdash passing in general, I still, it feels a little half baked.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:13.860
If you, if that makes sense.

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:19.820
Well, what I, what I think like, I like as the concept or even as, you know, the implementation,

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:26.840
like the actual fact that you kick the ball exactly where you, where your input is.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:30.740
And it's like, you, you, it's, it's, it feels real like that.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:34.820
Like kicking a real soccer ball, you know, you're, you're kicking in that direction and

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.240
your other player has to pick it up.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:38.220
And I think that's a really cool feature.

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:39.960
And, you know, it just depends on what you're used to and what you like.

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:43.760
But like, if you're, if you wipe clean all of your expectations of a soccer game,

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:45.700
personally, I like that.

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:51.020
I think that it doesn't play well with some other mechanics of this game, namely the fact

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:53.520
that you can only turn on exactly four points.

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:55.360
Yes. And I think that that's what ruins it.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:59.380
So like, I, there is frustration to it, but I think the frustration comes from like other

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.380
parts that don't work well with it.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:11.760
Yeah. tell where you are on the field.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:18.520
Um, you know, the direction you're going, but that, the sort of like 30 degree angle or

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:25.380
I don't even 25 degree angle off of, uh, like true North, I guess is what we'll say, uh,

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:34.200
makes it kind of difficult to, to move as you want to, because I kind of felt myself, uh,

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:38.220
trying to use my D pad directly.

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:45.300
Uh, like what I'm seeing on the screen, I'm trying to move my character into the screen

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:52.080
that way. But the thing about the way this game controls is like up or down just means up or down field

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:57.080
and not exactly like the, uh, perspective that you're seeing.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:15:59.300
Do you know what I'm saying?

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:05.320
Like the, how you're not actually going straight up or down at any point.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:07.440
Up the screen. But the D pad is giving you that.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:10.980
Yeah. You're almost always moving diagonals too.

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:15.200
You know, you're, you're kind of hooking either, uh, up into the left or up into the

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:20.000
right. Um, moving up is almost like moving right in this kind of angle.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:27.520
So, uh, I would also like to question just the amount of information they put on that

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:28.360
particular field.

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:33.100
You know, this is, this could be a plus for some people because a soccer field is rather

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:39.940
large, but I felt like not always knowing where players were in the, in the layout of

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:45.920
the actual like soccer field really hurt my experience because you do kind of have to sometimes

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:51.140
because the enemies are always coming close to you and they, uh, you know, they have a

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:55.300
sliding kick that is kind of devastating and doesn't have really a way to defend against

00:16:55.300 --> 00:17:00.020
it. Sometimes you have to get loose of that ball, but you don't know where your other players

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:04.320
are and you have to kind of just pass it off as a, well, I hope that they then give

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:06.320
me control of somebody who's close by.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:14.900
Yeah. I turned it over, um, in like the, uh, opponents and, and I was like, okay, now they're going

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:18.880
on a bit of a break and I'm sure my defenders will show up eventually.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:21.100
And now it's past midfield.

00:17:21.100 --> 00:17:24.460
And I'm like, there's definitely like two or three more guys on my team.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:29.140
And like, it turns out that they were like all in like the goal box.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:31.780
And I was like, what are we, what are we all doing here?

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:38.240
Like there's, there, there should be like maybe a couple like floating indicators on the, uh,

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:42.360
perimeter of the screen to sort of like show you where other guys are.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:46.640
I don't know. So there could be some quick fix, but that did happen to me a lot too.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:03.500
There's also, uh, you know, since we're talking about the slide tackles and stuff like that,

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:09.500
there's the ability for both the player and the opponent to do the slide tackle, but only

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:16.180
obviously if they are the, uh, defending, uh, opponent and only if they are within actual range

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:22.740
of the ball, you don't have a actual like slide tackle button that you can kind of, uh, you know,

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:26.320
anticipate and try to slide into the opponent.

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.620
You have to be already like lined up with them.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:33.140
And then that is how you will be able to perform the slide tackle.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:37.880
That is more like a, um, nifty way of stealing the ball.

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:41.100
I don't know. I felt like that should have just been a dedicated button.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:49.300
I agree. And that also sort of, uh, puts more like onus on the player of like, oh, you're going to

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:54.640
get like yellow carded or red carded if you spam this and end up doing the wrong thing.

00:18:54.820 --> 00:19:00.220
But it would also give you the opportunity to, to like do some really cool defensive plays

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:01.880
like in FIFA games.

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:09.040
Like there's, there's like definitely a really cool feeling when you come from way out and

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:13.020
just time it perfectly and end up just tipping the ball away.

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:20.260
I don't know. I think you're right that the whole like context based nature of this, of the tackle button

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:22.060
kind of removes a little bit of the drama.

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:27.160
Sean, you mentioned the penalties there and there's only two there's offsides and tripping,

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:33.000
but it's framed almost like in real soccer, you know, the referees don't always see the

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:37.760
play. And in this game, sometimes the penalties are just not called.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:43.400
It's up to you to decide if the risk of getting called out on the play is worth making it.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.720
Is that like in video games, right?

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:51.480
Is that actually a neat mechanic that the referees aren't perfect and that they won't always

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:54.220
call that play specifically in something like soccer too?

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:58.900
Or is this just a, I wish they would call it every time because it doesn't make any sense

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:02.380
to just randomly decide whether I should push my luck or not.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:11.260
Well, thankfully with offsides, like that is such a, uh, a bedrock rule in soccer that

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:13.280
it, it won't be randomized.

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:15.780
Like you're either offsides or you're on sides.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:21.980
And it's not like a football game where the penalty itself has to be abstracted into like

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:25.600
a mistake that a player is making like a false start or something.

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:29.940
Um, and it's actually like where you are positioning your players.

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:31.760
And if you did it wrong, you're going to get called.

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:34.100
So offsides I think is done fine.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:42.780
Um, whereas, uh, yeah, if it's something like, uh, tripping where you are mistiming, uh, your

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:48.600
slide tackle and hitting the player before the ball, I don't know if we're there yet with,

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:55.200
with this game because there's already kind of like so much of a mess in, in terms of like

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:59.440
when players get next to each other that it just turns into like this weird, like sprite

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:04.720
dance where they keep changing their perspective and the ball is kind of like close or not.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:07.080
And it's just not, it's not precise enough.

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:12.800
I think to whether you can have it be all the, it should be all the time or none of the

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:18.220
time. Um, in this case, like you can't really have those edge cases, um, like in more modern

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:22.120
games where they're just more, it's a more granular experience.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:37.840
The last intricacy of the gameplay I feel like we should discuss is that you have the ability

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:39.380
to headbutt the ball.

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:44.080
If you can, uh, you know, if the ball is low enough on contact where if it comes across

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:48.660
your player, you can press the A button to, uh, launch your head at it.

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:54.400
And it creates a cool graphic with the soccer ball to definitely feels like it has now bounced

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:59.040
off of someone's head and is heading in a more upwards direction, uh, in the, against

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:01.760
the opposite, uh, directions the ball was heading in.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:09.060
But I, I felt like this also required a certain timing that, or maybe a certain, you know,

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:14.020
level of the ball to be on the field that I couldn't quite see with the graphics.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:16.500
So when I was able to pull this off, it was really cool.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:20.020
I just thought like, I should have been able to do it maybe a little more.

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:22.140
It would have made the game a little more back and forth.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:28.520
Um, maybe it would have made it too easy to, uh, to answer the opponent's passes and kicks.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:31.120
But, um, that was just my personal feeling.

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:36.680
Yeah. I was only able to pull it off one time and I actually only had it, uh, used against me

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:37.840
like one or two times.

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:43.120
So it felt almost like inconsequential in the overall gameplay to me.

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:50.660
Yeah. And in terms of like how, how often you should be able to actually execute that, I don't

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:56.000
know how much of that is just the controls and not really knowing the timing or how much

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.720
of that is like in this game, there are stats.

00:22:58.940 --> 00:23:08.040
Like each player on each team, uh, has a different, uh, stat for any number of, uh, categories.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:10.160
Like, I mean, the most obvious is speed.

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:17.580
Um, but like there's like seven or eight different, uh, little stat categories that it's tracking

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:19.680
of like how good each player is.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:21.800
Um, so maybe that's part of it.

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:27.700
Um, and also why depending on which player you're, they all look the same.

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:32.920
So you're really just going by where they are on the field, um, to know which one you're

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:39.740
actually using, but maybe that's why it felt kind of hard to do, uh, over and over.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:44.780
I don't know. That's an interesting point because I feel like that is an overlooked part of the game

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:47.060
that they are, they are making you overlook.

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:51.260
As you mentioned, you're not, you're not necessarily keeping track of who is who they don't have

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:56.240
like player number icons next to the players when you're controlling them.

00:23:56.860 --> 00:24:00.700
So the only thing you really have to go off of is the position listed on these rating charts.

00:24:00.740 --> 00:24:07.720
They give ratings, uh, out of scores of, I believe one to 10 or zero to 10, even some

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:08.880
are zero to three as well.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:10.000
I think. Okay.

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:11.200
Got it. So, okay. Got it.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:14.200
So maybe some of them are like five to 10 and zero to three.

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:18.920
Like nobody scores a two on a 10 category because these are professional soccer.

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:26.000
Um, but you know, they do it for dribbling, kicking, speed, tackling ability, shoot ratio,

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:27.600
mark and ball keep.

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:31.600
And then the goalie has a whole separate, um, slew of things too that matter.

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:37.940
But I think it's interesting that these things are programmed, but they're kind of for the

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:40.680
actual like gameplay computer experience.

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.740
You know, like what the teams, just where the teams feel a little different in terms of

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:48.880
what the players are good at, but it's not something like, um, a base.

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:52.620
You know, like a football game where you actually think about the individual players stats to

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:54.980
be like, oh, well, this guy has a great arm.

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:59.900
So put them out in right field so he can throw into home, you know, or this guy's really strong.

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:02.320
So put them in, uh, the fourth position.

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:06.260
So this way, maybe he could hit a grand slam or have more players on base available to him.

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:10.800
It's very, um, it's very much for the actual programming of the game.

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:13.260
And then they probably just said, well, we have all these numbers.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:17.440
Might as well make it more useful and put it, uh, put that information somewhere within

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:19.200
the game. Yeah.

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:24.780
And I couldn't really tell because I only play it as two different teams and I think

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.760
the differences may be so granular.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:32.500
And also like the fact that each player, like it all just sort of melts together.

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:35.940
Um, cause you never know which position you're playing.

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:42.960
And all of these players are shifting positions so often that like the, like the, the forwards

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:48.000
may end up like what may end up in like a midfielder position because the ball's moving

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.840
around so much. Like you can never really tell.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:52.660
So it just, it didn't really have too much of an effect.

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:57.640
Maybe it made each, uh, opposing team feel a little different, but they all seem to average

00:25:57.640 --> 00:26:01.780
out because there wasn't too much discrepancy between stat lines.

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:03.220
I don't know. Yeah.

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:07.560
I mean, personally, again, maybe I just don't have the, the eye for it because I haven't

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:08.500
played this game enough.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:14.180
You know, I played two games also, but, um, you know, maybe, uh, like, like for me, it's

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:15.000
like they could have lied.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:17.580
All those numbers could just be a lie and I would never know.

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:19.760
Yeah. They put numbers on the screen.

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:22.260
Like I can't tell if those are actually accurate.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:24.520
I can't, I don't notice any difference between the other players.

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:27.820
They all look like the rollercoaster tycoon guests to me.

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:29.560
Yeah. That's true.

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:45.520
The one thing you have to give them credit for though, and feel free to tell me if this

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:47.720
wasn't your experience and I just suck at the game.

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:53.980
You have to give them credit for making soccer feel like how it is on TV, where it is impossible

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:55.080
to score goals.

00:26:55.120 --> 00:27:00.660
And it is a low scoring style game where when you do finally get the ball in the net, you're

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:04.980
thinking like, okay, I'm up by one and I feel like I can win this, you know, not like

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:07.240
the computer's going to answer back right away.

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:10.620
They, they, if they, you know, if they get the presentation part, right.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:14.480
Like we were always talking about with these Jaleco sports games, I feel like this part

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:17.020
was the most true, maybe not the most fun.

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:23.680
Right. I think, uh, nobody goes to, uh, play NHL 22 just this way.

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:25.900
They can miss the net a thousand times, right?

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:29.500
Like you want to, you want to score like seven times in a game and feel good about it.

00:27:29.860 --> 00:27:35.720
However, I think Jaleco again is just going for something more simulation strategy, skill

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:36.880
based for the time.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:42.900
And I think they've pulled it off pretty nicely here because getting, getting the ball, even

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:47.400
just over to the net and then dealing with the defenders and the goalie and getting it

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:51.140
by them does require, uh, you know, a pretty skillful setup.

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:53.740
And I'm not saying it requires advanced playing.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:57.300
Like you need to know all the ins and outs of the game in order to score goals.

00:27:57.380 --> 00:27:58.520
I scored goals.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:00.440
It just felt like they were earned.

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:05.460
Yeah. And I think that, uh, that's more so just the pace of the game.

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:13.220
Like in, in even in an NHL game, you're going to go from one end of the, of the ice to the

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:15.400
next, uh, pretty quickly.

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:20.180
Whereas, yeah, there's a, there are 11 guys that you have to get through if you're starting

00:28:20.180 --> 00:28:26.740
from your, your own baseline, um, before you can even attempt a shot on net.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:31.280
So some of it is just because it's soccer, but I also think that they did a pretty good

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:37.060
job in terms of balancing with like, if we're going to say all of the gameplay mechanics

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:41.380
that they've put together are like the way you want to do it.

00:28:41.900 --> 00:28:46.820
We also have two different modes here before we get into the third type of game.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:53.720
Uh, the first mode being world cup, where you play as one of 16 international teams and

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:59.640
true to the name, this actually starts at the pool round, which I feel like is very important

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:01.400
to be part of the world cup.

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:05.340
You don't just go into the single elimination tournament round.

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:11.500
You, you have to play, uh, for the points to even qualify to get in to the bracket style.

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:16.180
And I thought that was really a nice touch because the only other option is tournament

00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:19.720
mode where they've made it a single elimination tournament.

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:24.860
But to change it up even there, it's not the 16 international teams.

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:26.680
Now it's eight American cities.

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:30.300
And I feel like that is a nice touch to, to change this around.

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:32.140
And I'm not sure what it was in Japan.

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:37.420
They probably just made it 18, uh, Japanese cities, but I feel like that is a really cool

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:41.700
touch to both make those modes feel different in terms of teams you have access to, whether

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:46.920
we feel like they change all that much and to also make them feel different of like, there

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:52.540
is a purpose of going to tournament mode to maybe play a faster style tournament and then

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:57.060
world cup to be more methodical and, um, longer lasting.

00:29:57.720 --> 00:29:59.080
Yeah. I thought it was neat.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:04.420
And I always thought, I also thought it was funny that, um, in the manual, uh, because

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:10.960
they don't like, there aren't individual stat lines for each team that there's one for each

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:17.260
international team, but then for the American teams, they'll just like, this is France or

00:30:17.260 --> 00:30:25.500
Miami. Like each, the Miami American team is just a cookie cutter of the French team.

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:32.880
Yeah. Uh, so I thought that was, I just think it was cool that, um, that I guess that was like

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:35.600
the MLS teams at the time was the MLS a thing.

00:30:35.740 --> 00:30:36.760
I'm not entirely sure. That's what I was wondering.

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:38.360
Yeah. Is the MLS even a thing back then?

00:30:38.420 --> 00:30:42.280
But yeah, those, most of those cities do actually have MLS teams now.

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:45.320
So must be something about that.

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:51.900
Mm-hmm. And then the third mode is a shoot competition where you play as one of three star players.

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:53.400
I'm assuming these guys are real.

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:57.760
Um, I didn't see like a messy or anything like that, so I didn't recognize these names,

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.340
but I'm assuming they're real.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:05.760
And, uh, it's just a, basically a one-on-one with the goalie where you get five tries to get

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:07.920
past, uh, sorry, two defenders as well.

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:11.160
You have to first get past the two defenders and the goalie to score.

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:17.840
After those five tries, uh, you get five more tries to beat your previous score, or you can,

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:21.000
you know, hand off the controller to a friend and see if they can beat your score.

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:23.540
This is not a fully fledged mode.

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:30.720
This is more of just a way to play around with, uh, you know, a one V three style, uh, you

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:32.260
know, which, which you're going to run into in the real game.

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:38.260
I suppose it's not like they changed the actual layout of the game so that it's now, um, you

00:31:38.260 --> 00:31:41.900
know, first person or more, uh, side scrolling or something like that.

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:44.620
It's the same exact view just now one V three.

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:45.640
Yeah. It's closer.

00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:54.420
So your sprites are bigger and stuff, but, but I think that this, that this mode does like

00:31:54.420 --> 00:32:02.080
shine a light on, on the flaw I had with the other modes where, where your aim is just

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:07.580
to, you just don't have enough points on, on the circle on the 360 degrees around you

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:08.660
to shoot.

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:14.260
And I know you can like, you can, you can hook the ball or whatever, but it always feels

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:18.760
like, okay, I'm right now where I'm facing, I'll be shooting right at the goalie.

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:22.360
So I want to shoot a little bit to the left and then I turn a little bit to the left and

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:23.420
that's 90 degrees to the left.

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:26.000
And I'm just shooting completely like to the corner of the field now.

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:30.320
Like I, like I want, like, I don't know what the solution is, but like, I wish that even

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:35.940
if you just did like, if you hit up and right at the same time, you go kind of like Northeast

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:42.380
a little bit, but, but it doesn't seem unless I'm, unless I'm, uh, over exaggerating or

00:32:42.820 --> 00:32:48.540
misremembering. It feels like it's actually 90 degree changes, not even like 45 degree changes, which still

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:53.920
wouldn't be great. But like, I think that as much as I love the way that they're trying to do this, I almost

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:55.940
think like you kind of just need a joystick for that.

00:32:56.640 --> 00:33:03.320
And in addition to that, um, I think that there should have, there should have been like, however

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:06.980
long you, you hold down the button before releasing it.

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:12.960
Like that should indicate power where it just feels like every pass and every shot has the

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:16.680
same velocity, no matter like where you are.

00:33:16.820 --> 00:33:22.860
Like I want to do like a long lead pass up to my forward, but no, I'm just going to sort

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:28.300
of like kick it, uh, maybe, I don't know, 30 yards and then it just going to come to a

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:31.240
stop and whoever gets to there first gets to there.

00:33:31.700 --> 00:33:39.000
Um, so I think, yeah, but between, uh, although we've been kind of saying a lot of good things

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:46.160
about like how true of a simulation this game is for, for the NES, like it also is lacking

00:33:46.160 --> 00:33:52.200
in a lot of, uh, in a lot of places in terms of just the, the controls of the game.

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:54.100
And it can add a lot of frustration.

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:57.180
Yeah. I think that's important because we've been, you're right.

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:58.820
We've been hyping up the game a little bit.

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:03.080
I'm not sure when the time to have talked about, you know, the flaws would have been,

00:34:03.120 --> 00:34:06.720
I guess, you know, if you have flaws, list them in your essential games list vote, because

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:07.880
that's where it would really count.

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:14.780
But I would argue that the, the shoot competition mode as a whole is just kind of a nice bonus

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:15.700
thing to include.

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:17.640
Thankfully, it's not the actual game.

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:20.960
Uh, for me, this wasn't really something I played around with all that much.

00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:26.780
I, I do wonder if this should have been a mode where you had even more control over the

00:34:26.780 --> 00:34:30.860
ball in terms of spin and stuff like that, because as Joe mentioned, you are a little closer.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:36.300
So if you had any, if you had any way to have a little more fun with the type of shot you

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:39.540
could take on net, this would have been the place to put that in.

00:34:39.720 --> 00:34:44.280
Now with two players, the players can play against each other in the verse mode, but you

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:49.020
can actually play on the same team as well, which I think again, is just another great

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:54.000
feature. They're definitely, uh, thinking about, you know, a lot of quality of life improvements

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:58.020
and just things that we, we don't expect from NES sports games.

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:03.460
I think maybe that's why we've been so, uh, nice about the game is because yeah, these

00:35:03.460 --> 00:35:08.320
features might not be fully functional, uh, or even work all that well to begin with.

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:14.920
But I do think just the inclusion of these kinds of mindful modes make the game last longer,

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:18.920
you know, in terms of something that you buy and then you have to spend your time with.

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:24.940
I think a kid who gets goal might realize it's not a perfect game, but they have more than

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:29.920
enough time to learn the ins and outs of this game between world cup tournament, bringing

00:35:29.920 --> 00:35:35.060
a friend in to, you know, play on the same team, uh, doing the shoot competition and trying

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:38.600
to figure out the best way to get by two defenders and a goalie.

00:35:38.760 --> 00:35:43.140
Like there's, there's definitely a challenge here to all this stuff, but I, I praise them

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:44.720
for all the features they are including.

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:51.960
Oh yeah. There's a lot of, uh, there's a lot of, uh, potential being shown here in terms of like

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:53.720
the ideas that they're bringing to the genre.

00:35:54.520 --> 00:36:01.260
Um, and again, it's a running theme of this podcast is like, it mostly just lies in execution,

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:03.620
but we can tell what they were going for.

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:07.600
And I, I do appreciate the ideas they put forth.

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:15.520
Kind of want to jump back a little bit to, uh, when we were talking about the isometric

00:36:15.520 --> 00:36:18.960
view. Um, I know that, that you guys liked, liked it.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:26.060
I, I kind of didn't, I, I feel like maybe, you know, isometric view is like one of those

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:30.280
terms that like I've heard and used a million times and only just now I'm like questioning

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.000
if I actually know what it means.

00:36:32.300 --> 00:36:36.900
But what am I thought about it was always like, it's just this slightly, like the slightly

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:41.940
tilted view that kind of gives you this like faux 3d, right?

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:43.360
That's what that's the, that's the intent, right?

00:36:44.560 --> 00:36:50.460
Yeah. Um, but it also doesn't have like any, uh, depth.

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:54.540
So, um, like you don't have that like fading perspective.

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:58.960
It's just wherever you are on the screen, it's going to have that same perspective.

00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:06.220
Right. So I thought this didn't, didn't pull off what an isometric view is trying to do.

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:10.860
It does not look like there's, it does not look like there's depth in the sense that like,

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:17.740
like marble madness looks like, looks like there's Z space there, right?

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:24.120
This looks like it's a, it's a, it's an exact vertical soccer game that was tilted, not in

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:26.380
Z space, just tilted on like the XY axis.

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:30.520
And I think that's because there's just not enough detail on a soccer field.

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:32.040
There's not, there's not depth.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:34.820
The soccer field is too flat to make it look like that.

00:37:34.960 --> 00:37:38.300
So I just feel like this didn't, and maybe it's just, I'm not seeing it.

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:42.600
Like I'm looking at it, just not feel like something like marble madness does, or like

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:47.040
on the super Nintendo, like Kirby's dream course feels like it's in 3d space, even though it's

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:50.320
a 2d game. This, this just feels crooked.

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:57.320
Yeah. I, cause I, I actually called it like a faux isometric because it is like you're describing

00:37:57.320 --> 00:38:01.120
it more aptly than, than isometric would.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:07.300
Um, because if you're looking at the, if you're looking at the field, like if this were true

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:15.380
isometric, like the midfield circle would also, uh, look like it's sort of going into the screen

00:38:15.380 --> 00:38:17.680
a bit, whereas it really isn't in this.

00:38:17.780 --> 00:38:23.740
And so that all that's kind of like plays into like your mind is kind of confused.

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:29.060
Like my mind gets kind of confused when I look at it, um, because the field doesn't, it looks

00:38:29.060 --> 00:38:29.860
kind of off.

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:38.040
I think that it was done well enough to, um, to be like relatively comfortable to play with,

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:39.740
which is why I was sort of praising it.

00:38:39.800 --> 00:38:43.700
But I do agree that like it, it's not perfect.

00:38:44.320 --> 00:38:50.480
Yeah. I think that the benefit that I see from it being this way is that you now the horizontal,

00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:56.720
the, the horizontal, uh, perspective of the field is going corner to corner, which I guess

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:01.760
gives you more space horizontally, at least in those corners, although it does then taper off

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:03.080
at the, at the other two corners.

00:39:03.260 --> 00:39:09.560
But, but the, the, the negative is just that it feels funky because like you said before,

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:15.440
like up is really kind of like up left, you know, and like, and so like, I, I wonder if

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:20.440
it was worth them kind of tilting it like that rather than just having like a, like an overhead

00:39:20.440 --> 00:39:21.760
view, like we've seen other times.

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:29.140
Well, the problem with the overhead view that I have with the NES soccer game from the original

00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:35.180
black box series is that that, I don't know, that kind of simplifies the whole like soccer

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:36.980
experience to a level.

00:39:36.980 --> 00:39:41.220
And maybe that's just the gameplay itself, but I didn't feel like that was exciting.

00:39:41.460 --> 00:39:46.100
That, that felt very stiff and rigid and the camera only moved when you got to like the

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:50.940
end of the screen and then it moved to the entire next portion of the soccer field.

00:39:51.100 --> 00:39:52.940
That felt a little less dynamic.

00:39:53.260 --> 00:39:58.780
Um, I definitely think that just having two different colored green lines across the screen

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:03.720
and the white box around the perimeter isn't interesting enough either for, um, what amounts

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:08.800
to most of the game design here, but I'd, I'd argue that I thought it was a step up from

00:40:08.800 --> 00:40:10.300
what NES soccer was offering.

00:40:10.460 --> 00:40:14.600
And I think that's like the key word there is, is this looks more dynamic.

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:18.840
Um, that was sort of how I was going to, uh, describe it as well.

00:40:19.220 --> 00:40:25.520
Um, and I think it's, it's less so about the field itself and more just the view that you

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:26.440
have of the players.

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:34.180
Like, I think that there's a little bit more naturalism and clarity seeing your players,

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:40.720
um, from this perspective than you would from if, even if you went from end to end up and

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:42.500
down instead of left and right.

00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:51.460
So I can see both sides of this, but I definitely see your, uh, you're not liking this perspective.

00:40:52.540 --> 00:40:54.620
Yeah. I mean, like, again, I like what they were going for.

00:40:54.800 --> 00:40:58.660
